  "question": "Two identical firms compete in prices (Bertrand) in an infinitely repeated game. Market demand is P = 100 - Q, marginal cost is constant at c = 20, and fixed costs are zero. The firms adopt a grim-trigger strategy: each period they collusively set the monopoly price P = 60 and split output and profit equally; if any firm deviates, both permanently revert to the one-shot Bertrand-Nash outcome. A deviator marginally undercuts the collusive price, captures the entire market for one period, and then receives the punishment payoff forever after. Both firms share a common discount factor delta in (0, 1). Which of the following correctly states the minimum discount factor delta* that sustains collusion as a subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium, together with its key real-world implication?",
  "options": {
    "A": "delta* ~ 0: any positive discount factor sustains collusion, because an infinitesimal price cut yields only an infinitesimal gain (epsilon x Q), so deviation is never profitable.",
    "B": "delta* = 0.50: collusion is self-enforcing through rational punishment threats even absent explicit communication, implying that supracompetitive 'parallel pricing' can arise as equilibrium behavior — so antitrust analysis must attend to market structure (patience, interaction frequency, price transparency), not merely evidence of agreements.",
    "C": "delta* ~ 0.90: the relevant punishment is reversion to the Cournot-Nash quantity equilibrium rather than Bertrand reversion, since the Folk theorem requires quantity-based Nash reversion.",
    "D": "delta* = 1 (never sustained): because the one-shot Bertrand game has a unique Nash equilibrium with zero profits, the Folk theorem cannot support positive-profit collusion in the infinitely repeated game."
  },
  "correct_answer": "B",
  "explanation": {
    "derivation": "Monopoly benchmark: MR = 100 - 2Q = 20 gives Q_M = 40, P_M = 60, pi_M = (60 - 20) x 40 = 1600, so each firm earns 800 per period under collusion. One-shot Bertrand-Nash equilibrium: P = MC = 20, pi = 0. Deviation: undercutting 60 by epsilon captures the whole market at a near-monopoly margin, yielding approximately 1600 for one period and 0 thereafter. The incentive constraint is 800/(1 - delta) >= 1600, i.e., delta >= 1/2. Equivalently, delta* = (pi_deviation - pi_collusion)/(pi_deviation - pi_punishment) = (1600 - 800)/1600 = 0.5. The grim-trigger profile is subgame perfect because the punishment path simply repeats the stage-game Nash equilibrium, which is an equilibrium of every continuation subgame.",
    "why_other_options_are_wrong": {
      "A": "A marginal undercut (to 60 - epsilon) wins the entire market at a near-collusive margin, so the one-period deviation gain is ~1600, not epsilon x Q; a strictly positive critical discount factor is therefore required.",
      "C": "The stem specifies permanent reversion to the Bertrand (price) outcome. Cournot reversion would instead yield pi_i = ((a - c)/(n+1))^2 = (80/3)^2 ~ 711 per firm and delta* ~ 800/(1600 - 711) ~ 0.90, but that is not the stated punishment, and the Folk theorem imposes no quantity-reversion requirement.",
